[cot:thought] Drafting a Q3 concentration post that distinguishes itself from the 2026 version.
[cot:thought] The draft will anchor
[cot:thought] on Q3 vintage context: 76.66% consent rate, November 15 deadline. It will explore authority market share across IMF/IBRD votes, Executive Board chairs, multilateral bank subscriptions, UN assessed contributions, and NATO burden-sharing.
[cot:thought] A radar or heatmap on blocking coalition control is planned. Distinct dashboard chart types will prevent cloning.
[cot:thought] The dashboard will
[cot:thought] use stacked top-k waterfalls, radar charts, heatmaps, and dual-axis consent-gap plots. The governance stack will cover six authority buckets: IMF votes, IBRD votes, UN regular budget assessments, NATO defense spending, UNSC veto, and MDB capital. ADB and AIIB will be contrasted.
[cot:thought] The post will center
[cot:thought] on a paradox: relative concentration has held steady while consent climbed 3.88 points.
[cot:thought] The data module will highlight IMF Top-1 at 16.5% and Top-3 at 28.7%, alongside US dominance in UN assessments and NATO spending.
[cot:thought] A chair and constituency concentration metric will be added, plus an effectiveness gate showing that US alone blocks at 85% consent.
[cot:thought] The dashboard will use a grouped ComposedChart for multi-institution Top-1/Top-3 comparisons and a Lorenz-style Area+Line curve for IMF concentration.
[cot:thought] The dashboard will
[cot:thought] include a horizontal bar for blocking coalitions, a timeline area chart for consent versus frozen shares, a pie or donut chart for UN assessments or NATO shares, and a scatter plot for vote versus contribution. Controls will let users switch between stack, ladder, coalitions, and assessments views, pick top-1, top-3, or top-5 metrics, filter by institution,
[cot:thought] and toggle an equal line. The next step is to review the ConsumerFinanceMarketsConcentrationDashboard pattern and verify recharts RadarChart support before shipping the data module.
